{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel wept.</b> But concerning Aaron it says (Numbers 20:29) ״כֹּל בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — all the house of Israel. Our Rabbis of blessed memory (Avot de-Rabbi Natan, chapter 12) gave the reason: because he was a lover of peace, etc." "<b>And Rabbi</b> Abraham ibn Ezra wrote that all the house of Israel wept in honor of Moses while he was alive, and one should not believe his interpretation, for one must not cast aspersion on the good deed that Israel performed — [to suggest] that they did it for Moses while he was alive, yet when Moses himself died they hid from the warmth of mourning him." "<b>Or perhaps</b> one may give a reason: concerning Aaron they did not know of his death before he died, and they suddenly saw that he had died — and such a thing arouses every heart to open the wellspring of its tears. Not so with Moses, for they already knew of his death, and in all his words and reproofs that he delivered he would say to them (Deuteronomy 4:22) ״כִּי אָנֹכִי מֵת וְגוֹ׳ אֵינֶנִּי עֹבֵר״ etc., and therefore the heat of his eulogy was not as with Aaron." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that the death of Aaron affected them more, because immediately the clouds of glory departed and their protective shelter was removed, and this caused the attack of the Canaanite, etc. (Numbers 21:1). Therefore all Israel together were stirred to weep. Not so with the death of Moses — although the clouds, etc., were taken, they were not aroused over this, for they were also absorbed in the joy of entering the good land." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that at the death of Moses they immediately saw a new thing — that the Shekhinah rested upon Joshua — and they took comfort. It is like one who lost a pearl and found another in its place; even if it does not compare, nevertheless he takes comfort in it. Therefore they did not all weep, and this is why Scripture juxtaposes to the statement ״וַיִּבְכּוּ״ the statement ״וִיהוֹשֻׁעַ בִּן נוּן״ etc. Not so with the death of Aaron: they lost a pearl and found nothing with which to take comfort. For although Elazar was appointed in his place as High Priest, nevertheless one does not weep over the lack of a high priesthood, but rather over the loss of his personal beneficial qualities, for which they had no replacement."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:44.090091008Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}